Z||000000||fieroX||11-10-2001||01:48 PM||||I saw a picture in an old kit car mag of mine of a ferrari kit that had some awesome flushmount headlights, so I decided to take the idea and put it to my fiero in photoshop. Im not very good at photoshop yet, but this didnt take very long.  Im thinking about making a set for my car that will look similiar.  And yes the hood will require modification.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fieroX.com/flushmounts2.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-10-2001||04:57 PM||||That looks good.  Get rid of the rivets and make the headlamps smaller and you'd be all set.  I like the size of the buckets.  You'd need to cut away part of the hood, but I don't think it would make much of a difference.  Hmmm...Just think what a little bit of aluminum sheeting and a Dremel could do.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroX||11-10-2001||06:24 PM||||those arent supposed to be rivets, they are supposed to be stainless screws, and there will be a lexan cover that goes over the bucket.  The screws are removable so you can access the headlights, and they will use standard gm square headlights that most gm's used in the 80's.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Reality||11-10-2001||07:25 PM||||See if you can get Rogergarrison to post a pic of the headlights on his Ferrari kit car.  I think that is what you are after.  He made them himself and they use the clear cover like you mentioned.  They look very good and would lend themselves to a project like you are contemplating.||||reg||3
